\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:future-work}
The proposed OU--III quaternion MEKF couples attitude estimation to a
third-order translational chain with adaptive integral-state regularization.
Under the paired ten-seed JONSWAP protocol, its seed-wise mean normalized
vertical-displacement error is
$\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedStd\%$ of
$H_s$, compared with
$\OUValidationOUIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIINormalizedStd\%$ for
OU--II.  The paired difference is
$\OUValidationNormalizedDifference$ percentage point with bootstrap 95\%
interval
$[\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceLow,\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceHigh]$.
The PM--Stokes ensemble gives the same qualitative result.  The improvement is
primarily vertical: OU--III has higher three-dimensional displacement error in
\OUValidationThreeDHigherCount{} of the
\OUValidationThreeDScenarios{} tested scenarios, so the added regularization
should not be interpreted as a general navigation-position improvement.

The adaptation experiments narrow the mechanism.  Keeping $r_S$ adaptive while
freezing $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ reproduces nearly all of the vertical benefit,
whereas adapting the OU parameters with $r_S$ fixed contributes little to the
vertical score.  Online tuning is therefore most strongly supported as a means
of adapting the integral-state regularization to large sea-state mismatch and
to changing conditions.  The sensitivity study is consistent with this
interpretation: direct vertical sensitivity to $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ is weak
around the nominal point because the latent acceleration is observed directly
at high rate, while $r_S$ governs the weakly observed low-frequency drift
channel.  Low-motion and rapid-transition cases bound the empirical claim to
the tested signal-to-noise levels and transition construction.

Appendix~\ref{app:iss-stability} establishes nominal local stability of the
21-state Live error dynamics under bounded noncollinear reference geometry,
bounded measurement gaps, exact $T_S$ spacing for the four integral-state
pseudo-updates used on each translational proof horizon, finite positive
covariance bounds, and finite accelerometer-bias correlation time.  The result
is local and applies between hard resets; startup, re-lock, gating, general
sample-time jitter of the nominal $S$ deadlines, and arbitrary ocean-model
mismatch are outside its scope.

The present evidence remains primarily simulation based.  The only method run
under the full paired inferential protocol is OU--II, so the paper establishes
an architectural increment rather than a definitive ranking against published
heave-estimation practice.  A conventional spectral double-integration
baseline and a current published heave estimator should be evaluated under the
same paired protocol.  The ESP32--S3 exercise establishes source portability,
not timing, memory, power, or measurement accuracy.  Future validation should
therefore add independent sea states and update rates, a continuously evolving
non-stationary spectrum, quantitative embedded-resource measurements, a
varying-temperature calibration/lag experiment, and an external physical
displacement reference from a motion platform, wave tank, GNSS/optical system,
or commercial MRU.
